# Sex, gender and sexuality

The language around sex, gender and sexuality changes all the time. Stay in touch with the communities you are writing for.

People hold strong and differing opinions about this language. This page helps you get started, but the best thing is to test your content and services with the people who use them.

Mention sex, gender or sexuality only if it is relevant, for example to point people to the information or help they need.

Example: a profile for a smart scale that needs a person’s sex to estimate body fat.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| Are you a man or a woman? | Sex, used to estimate body fat |

The label says why the information is needed. The wording in this example is made up.

## When to use “sex” and when to use “gender”

Many people think that sex and gender are the same, but they mean different things. Be clear about the difference.

### Sex

Sex is biological (male or female). It is based not only on the genes we inherit, but also on how our external and internal sex and reproductive organs work and respond to hormones. Sex is the label recorded when a baby’s birth is registered.

Use “sex” or, better still, the body part associated with biological sex when you write about something that is specific to sex.

#### Sex assigned or registered at birth

Use the phrase “sex assigned at birth” when you talk about trans health and gender dysphoria, because this is the language the audience uses. In other cases, use “the sex someone was registered with at birth”. User research shows that most people understand this better, because it refers to an actual event.

#### Intersex

Use “intersex” in some content about people with differences in sex development (DSD), because some people prefer it to DSD.

DSD involves genes, hormones and reproductive organs, including genitals. A person’s physical sex development, internally, externally or both, can be different from most other people’s.

Most people with DSD have a clear sex that is registered at birth. Some adults with DSD prefer the term “intersex” to DSD but may want to keep their legal sex as male or female. Other people see “intersex” as distinct from male and female.

### Gender

Gender is more complex. It refers to our internal sense of who we are and how we see and describe ourselves.

Someone may see themselves as a man, a woman or neither (non-binary). Being non-binary can mean having no gender, a different gender, or being in between genders. Gender can be fixed or fluid. Some people identify with a gender opposite to the sex they were registered with.

Use the word “gender” when you are:

- discussing the social idea or identity, as opposed to biological sex, for example when writing about gender dysphoria or transgender health and social care
- writing about a survey or report based on gender, such as gender diversity
- writing about the results of a national census where there is a question about gender identity as well as sex, to identify the trans (including non-binary) population

## Gender neutral language

Make content gender neutral as far as possible. In general, word your content to avoid masculine and feminine pronouns (“he” or “she”). Use “you” where appropriate, and sometimes “they” when you need a gender-neutral pronoun, unless this is confusing.

Example: a notification.

| Before | After |
| --- | --- |
| He arrived home at 5:30pm. | Sam arrived home at 5:30pm. |
| Her bedroom light is still on. | The light in Robin’s bedroom is still on. |
| The guest has arrived. Let him in. | A guest has arrived. They are at the front door. |

The names in these examples are made up.

Avoid asking people for their title, such as Mr, Miss, Mrs or Ms.

## Transgender

Use “trans” as an umbrella term for people whose current gender identity or way of expressing their gender differs from the sex they were registered with at birth. Some, but not all, trans people want to transition (change) socially or medically or both.

Use “trans woman” for someone who was registered male at birth and now identifies as a woman. Use “trans man” for someone who was registered female at birth and now identifies as a man.

Use “trans woman” or “trans man” in content about the particular health needs of trans people, for example screening or treatments that trans people need to be aware of.

Otherwise, leave out the word “trans” and refer to men and women, if relevant.

Note: use “sex assigned at birth” when you write for a trans audience. Read [Sex assigned or registered at birth](#sex-assigned-or-registered-at-birth).

## Sexuality

Use language about sexuality when it helps to point people to the information or help they need.

For example, when you talk about specific sexual health services or sexual health content, use words such as:

- lesbian
- gay
- bisexual
- men who have sex with men (this includes men who may not identify as gay)

For wording that does not assume who is reading, read [Do not assume who is reading](../writing-guidelines/clear-language.html#wg-060).
